  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nModels have trended the best shower and thunderstorm chances \nsouth of our area tonight and Saturday morning, closer to the \nhigher instability and stronger lift over Illinois. Still, \nmodest ascent and some warm/moist advection should allow a few \nscattered showers to pop up. Highest chance of a shower (30% \nchance) will be across central into east-central Wisconsin, but \nmost locations are expected to remain dry into the afternoon \nhours Saturday.\n\nA cold front will approach Saturday evening, moving southeastward\nacross the area overnight into Sunday morning as a shortwave \ntrough clips the northern Great Lakes. Anomalously high\nprecipitable water values (1.8 plus inches) should allow \nscattered showers and isolated thunderstorms to develop. \nStrongest forcing for ascent passing north of the area should be\nsomewhat detached from the strongest instability south of the \narea, so aside from isolated heavy downpours, not expecting \nwidespread heavy rain with this system.\n\nBehind the front, quiet weather with lower dewpoints expected \nfrom Sunday evening through Monday night.\n\nThe next period of active weather is expected late Tuesday through\nWednesday, with a shortwave trough rotating around the longwave\ntrough over Hudson Bay. Sufficient moisture and instability are \nexpected to be in place, yielding a high chance of showers and \nthunderstorms. Some hint of jet coupling over Wisconsin so we \nwill need to watch for the potential for heavy rainfall amounts.",
